      "inputTokens": 129,
      "outputTokens": 864,
      "costUsd": 0.0078405,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's cos x, from zero to pi over two, with eight thin rectangles standing against it. Let's give the curve a meaning. A storm is easing off. cos x is the rain rate, in centimetres per hour, x hours after it starts to ease. At the start, it's one centimetre an hour. By pi over two hours, about an hour and a half, the rain has stopped. So how much rain fell into the gauge? The rate keeps changing, so we can't just multiply rate by time. But adding up thin rectangles gives an estimate, and refining them makes it better."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-i06/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1014,
