      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 117,
      "outputTokens": 845,
      "costUsd": 0.0076635,
      "pronunciationText": "Take one thin strip of time, with width delta x. Delta x just means a small change in x, here a short stretch of time. Over that short time, the rate hardly changes. Call the rate f of x. So the rain that falls is about rate times time. On the graph, that's the rectangle's height times its width, its area. Centimetres per hour, times hours, gives centimetres. So each rectangle's area estimates the rain that fell during its strip. That's the change in the quantity over one small step."
    },
